<section>
    <h2>More than a click</h2>
    <p>
        <code>ClickAsync</code> has carried you this far, but people use a mouse in more ways than one: they
        double-click to edit a cell, right-click for a menu, drag a card to another column, hover to see a tooltip.
        Each of those fires different events on the page, and a page that listens for one won't answer another.
        Playwright can send all of them, and if it really comes to it, it can move the pointer itself.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Double-clicks, right-clicks and hovering</h2>
<pre><code>await cell.DblClickAsync();                                        // fires dblclick
await card.ClickAsync(new() { Button = MouseButton.Right });       // fires contextmenu
await link.ClickAsync(new() { Modifiers = new[] { KeyboardModifier.ControlOrMeta } });
await avatar.HoverAsync();                                         // shows the tooltip</code></pre>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Two clicks aren't a double-click.</strong> Calling <code>ClickAsync</code> twice sends two single
        clicks, and the page's <code>dblclick</code> event never fires. <code>DblClickAsync</code> sends the real thing.
    </p>
    <p>
        Every click takes options: <code>Button</code>, <code>ClickCount</code>, <code>Modifiers</code>, and
        <code>Position</code> to click a particular point inside the element instead of its centre.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Dragging</h2>
    <p>
        Dragging from one element to another is one line. Playwright presses on the first, moves, and lets go on the second:
    </p>
<pre><code>await Page.GetByText("Write tests").DragToAsync(Page.GetByLabel("Done"));</code></pre>
    <p>
        When there's no element to drop on, only a spot (a slider, a drawing canvas, a crop handle), drive the mouse
        yourself. <code>Page.Mouse</code> works in page coordinates, and <code>BoundingBoxAsync</code> tells you where
        an element is:
    </p>
<pre><code>var box = (await slider.BoundingBoxAsync())!;
var y = box.Y + box.Height / 2;

await Page.Mouse.MoveAsync(box.X, y);                      // the left end
await Page.Mouse.DownAsync();
await Page.Mouse.MoveAsync(box.X + box.Width * 0.75, y, new() { Steps = 5 });
await Page.Mouse.UpAsync();                                // three-quarters of the way</code></pre>
    <p>
        <code>Steps</code> sends the move as several smaller ones, for pages that only react while the pointer is
        moving. Then check the result the way a user would: <code>ToHaveAttributeAsync("aria-valuenow", "75")</code>.
    </p>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>The mouse doesn't look before it presses.</strong> <code>Page.Mouse</code> presses wherever you
        tell it, on the visible window, even if the slider has scrolled off screen or is tucked under a sticky
        header, and then the press lands on the header. <code>HoverAsync</code> with a <code>Position</code> moves the
        same mouse but checks first: it scrolls the slider into view and waits until nothing covers that spot.
    </p>
<pre><code>var width = (await slider.BoundingBoxAsync())!.Width;

await slider.HoverAsync(new() { Position = new() { X = 1, Y = 10 } });            // the left end
await Page.Mouse.DownAsync();
await slider.HoverAsync(new() { Position = new() { X = width * 0.75f, Y = 10 } }); // three-quarters
await Page.Mouse.UpAsync();</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>The keyboard</h2>
    <p>
        <code>FillAsync</code> sets a field in one go. When a page reacts to each key (shortcuts, autocomplete,
        games), press keys instead:
    </p>
<pre><code>await editor.PressAsync("Control+Enter");               // a shortcut on one element
await search.PressSequentiallyAsync("dragon", new() { Delay = 50 });   // typed one key at a time
await Page.Keyboard.PressAsync("Escape");                // whatever has focus</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>The Tinkerer's Workbench</h2>
    <p>
        The foundry's furnace won't light until its clockwork is serviced, and nothing on the bench answers a plain
        click. Loosen the cover's four bolts, do the job scratched inside it, set the furnace dial to the exact
        temperature, and fire it. Ten seconds.
    </p>
</section>
